      "inputTokens": 82,
      "outputTokens": 617,
      "costUsd": 0.005594,
      "pronunciationText": "Here's a surprise. The narrower the interval, the taller the density has to be. What if X is uniform on a short interval, from zero to a half? The rectangle is only ay half wide. So to have area one, its height must be two. A density of two is fine. It isn't a probability. Probability is area, and the area is still exactly one."
    },
