      "outputTokens": 944,
      "costUsd": 0.0085665,
      "pronunciationText": "When is this a good model? When nothing makes one part of the interval more likely than another. Turning up at a random moment for a bus that comes exactly every ten minutes fits. The rounding error, when you round a length to the nearest centimetre, often fits too, anywhere from minus a half to a half, if the lengths aren't bunched near whole numbers. But a reaction time doesn't. Those times bunched up in the middle, as the histogram showed. A flat model would give the extreme times far too much probability. And a bus wait doesn't fit either if you've checked the timetable, because then you'd arrive just before the bus."
